The game isn't up to today's standarts on many metrics:
* The graphics are horrifying;
* Controls are beyound counterintuitive;
* The game requires time investment to learn its quirks, otherwise it's difficult to stay alive for more than a couple of minutes;
* Plot is there, but it isn't engaging or innovative and is sometimes odd in the worst sense;
* Bugs are present aplenty.
However, for some people, including myself, these metrics aren't important. The game excells in many things, and for some of us this greatness outweighs the weak spots.
* The world is believable and unusual. The game takes place in an uncommon setting of an extremely large prison colony, and as a result the interactions between characters are interesting to observe. The history of the colony is also amusing. There are no obvious black or white characters, everyone persues their own goals -- that is, if one doesn't take into account demons, which are obviously evil.
* The location design provides for a great sense of exploration. Each cave, each hill has something to show, and many walls are climbable -- and more often than not interesting things await on top of them. And when there's loot, it's not just "another 30 frost damage ebony axe" as is common for the majority of RPGs, but leaves the player sifting with excitement through the newfound stuff: "What does this scroll do? Can I wield this sword? What is this lute for? That's a neat ring -- I wonder whose was it!"
* The atmosphere is quirky in a good way. The game sometimes fools around and has time for humour, which, however, is quite dark. The whole game is like that -- it is dark without overdoing it. Just look at the color pallete to get a sense of what it's like. And when the game does joke, it only deepens the darkness.

